# .mailmap
#
# Normalizes names and addresses for the tools that read history:
# `git shortlog`, `git log` and `git blame` all apply this file, so a person
# who committed under more than one address appears once.
#
# It records no contribution and assigns no responsibility.  `AUTHORS` is the
# record of who took part and `MAINTAINERS` is who reviews what; this file only
# makes the history agree with them.
#
# ## Writing an entry
#
# One entry per line.  The **left** side is what a reader should see; the
# **right** side is what a commit says.  The forms, in the order they are
# useful:
#
#   <proper@example.com> <old@example.com>
#       Only the address is wrong: show the new one, keep the name the commit
#       carried.
#
#   Proper Name <proper@example.com> <old@example.com>
#       The address is wrong and the name belongs to it: show both new ones.
#
#   Proper Name <proper@example.com> Old Name <old@example.com>
#       Both are wrong *and* the old name is used by somebody else, so the
#       entry must match the pair and not the name alone.
#
#   Proper Name <proper@example.com>
#       The one form with no right side: every commit from this address gets
#       this name, whatever name it carried.
#
# Names and addresses match case-insensitively.  `#` starts a comment, blank
# lines are ignored.
#
# ## Checking an entry
#
# `git check-mailmap` reads this file and answers the canonical form, so a
# mapping is known to be in effect before it is committed:
#
#   git check-mailmap 'Old Name <old@example.com>'
#
# An entry is added in the same commit as the contribution that needs it.
#
# This history carries one address, so there is nothing to normalize yet.  What
# is here is that address in its canonical form — the same line `AUTHORS`
# carries — so the file has a real entry to be checked against rather than a
# shape to be trusted.

